  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1029 PM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nRinse and repeat. Above normal temperatures and dry conditions\nwill persist as an anomalous 592dam ridge remains parked over the\ncentral CONUS. Chilly mornings with lows in the 40s to mid 50s\nand warm afternoons with highs in the mid 70s to lower 80s will\nremain the story. A very dry airmass also remains fixed over the\nMississippi Valley with a very dry air column present as PWATs\nremain around the 25th percentile for this time of the year, 0.5-\n0.7\". It goes without saying, this airmass will not be able to\nsustain any precipitation over the next several days. Through\nmidweek, very efficient diurnal mixing will result in afternoon\ndewpoints in the 40s. A combination of low relative humidity, dry\nfuels, and warm temperatures will result in elevated fire weather\nconcerns through this period.\n\nBy midweek, upper-level southwest flow will begin to build across\nthe region. In terms of precipitation and temperatures, little to\nno change is to note as we remain dry and above normal. As\npersistent ridging flattens, dewpoints may be able to surge ever\nso slightly Wednesday through the end of the workweek, however,\nfire weather concerns will continue given dry vegetation and\nworsening drought conditions. To answer the question, when will\nwe see rain next, late Friday, a subtropical jet will begin to\nstrengthen over the Southern Plains. This will finally promote an\nend to our persistent dry spell as moisture begins to increase\nover the Middle Mississippi Valley this weekend. Latest LREF\nguidance has a 40% chance of exceeding 0.5\" of precipitation and\na 50-60% chance of exceeding 0.25\". Bottom line, we're not\nlooking at a washout, but we'll take what we can get with extreme\ndrought (D3) and exceptional drought (D4) over portions of the\nMid-South.\n\nAEH",
